{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Sing</b> — They say: these [words] 'sing to Elohim.'" "<b>Selah</b> — like סלסלוהו (extol Him), in the manner of הללו (praise)." "<b>Upon the plains</b> — a name for the heavens. And Rabbi Moses said: the heavens are so called because they are broad, and he also said that the word סלה (selah) is like סולו סולו המסילה (raise up, raise up the highway). And the meaning of 'who rides upon the plains' is: he who pilgrimages to the House of HaShem, who rides upon a plain [ערבה]. And the correct [interpretation]: that it is 'who rides in the heaven of heavens of old.' And because the motions are three: one rises like fire, and corresponding to it is the mountain; and the second descends like the earth, and this corresponds to the valley and the plain; and the middle one is the motion of the sphere; and the bet of ביה (in it) serves." "And the meaning is: by this name exalt Him and raise Him up, not as many supposed, for the bet is primary, and so they do with all the letters; thus 'for in it [ביה] is the strength of the Nestor [i.e., the cross].'"], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 68, :notes {3 "The phrase תוקף הנצוח is obscure; read hyper-literally. 'Nestor' (נצוח) likely refers to a Christological symbol associated with the bet of ביה, possibly a polemical allusion. The syntax is compressed and possibly textually difficult."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:33:08.224915039Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}